> inverse 3 mod 11
3^-1 = 4 mod 11
  how: extended Euclid: 4*3 - 1*11 = 1
  check: 3 * 4 = 12 = 1*11 + 1
> inverse 4 mod 8
4 has no inverse mod 8: gcd(4, 8) = 4 > 1
  4 * 2 = 8 = 0 mod 8, so 4 is a zero divisor; a*x = 1 would give 2 = 2 * 4 * x = 0
  the units mod 8 are the residues coprime to 8 (phi(8) = 4 of them)
> inverse 10 mod 17
10^-1 = 12 mod 17
  how: extended Euclid: 12*10 - 7*17 = 1
  check: 10 * 12 = 120 = 7*17 + 1
> inverse -3 mod 11
  note: -3 = 8 mod 11
8^-1 = 7 mod 11
  how: extended Euclid: 7*8 - 5*11 = 1
  check: 8 * 7 = 56 = 5*11 + 1
> order 3 mod 7
ord(3) = 6 mod 7
  how: the order divides lambda(7) = 6; 3^6 = 1 and 3^(6/2) = 6, 3^(6/3) = 2
  3 is a primitive root mod 7 (its order is phi(7) = 6)
  additive order: 7
  check: stepping through the powers, 3^k = 1 first at k = 6
> order 2 mod 8
2 has no multiplicative order mod 8: gcd(2, 8) = 2, so no power of it is 1
  additive order: 4 (8 / gcd(2, 8))
> order 3 mod 11
ord(3) = 5 mod 11
  how: the order divides lambda(11) = 10; 3^5 = 1 and 3^(5/5) = 3
  not a primitive root: phi(11) = 10
  additive order: 11
  check: stepping through the powers, 3^k = 1 first at k = 5
> order 2 mod 1000000007
ord(2) = 500000003 mod 1000000007
  how: the order divides lambda(1000000007) = 1000000006; 2^500000003 = 1 and 2^(500000003/500000003) = 2
  not a primitive root: phi(1000000007) = 1000000006
  additive order: 1000000007
  check: 2^500000003 = 1 by repeated squaring
> order 1 mod 1
ord(0) = 1 mod 1
  how: the order divides lambda(1) = 1; 0^1 = 1
  0 is a primitive root mod 1 (its order is phi(1) = 1)
  additive order: 1
  check: stepping through the powers, 0^k = 1 first at k = 1
